  "live_records": {
    "q-4825": {
      "dom_id": "step-q4825",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x$ and $g(x)=9-2x,$ find $(fg)(-1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{2}+5(-1)=-3 \\\\ g(-1) & =9-2(-1)=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-1)=f(-1)g(-1)=-3\\cdot 11=-33\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(2{x}^{2}+5x)(9-2x) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+45x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-1) & =-4(-1)^{3}+8(-1)^{2}+45(-1) \\\\  & =4+8-45 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
