    "q-66425": {
      "dom_id": "step-q66425",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-6$ and $g(x)=-2x+2,$ find  $(f/g)(3).$",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(3),$ we find $f(3)$ and $g(3),$ and then divide the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(3)$ and $g(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2(3)^{2}-6=12 \\\\ g(3) & =-2(3)+2=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(3)=\\frac{f(3)}{g(3)}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{-2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(3) & =\\frac{2(3)^{2}-6}{-2(3)+2}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
